\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Three functions meet the positive eigenvector}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Assume the Rayleigh hypothesis, as \ref{fdip-012Y} does for
the rescaled matrix of \ref{fdip-012U}. Let the positive eigenvector
given by \ref{fdip-011X} have coordinates in that rescaled
basis. The constant function, the
[double-coset kernel](fdip-012C) #{B}, and
the indicator of the stabilizer each have positive standard
dot product with that eigenvector. The stabilizer indicator
is not a scalar multiple of the eigenvector. The other two
products do not decide whether those functions are
eigenvectors.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-012U} makes the rescaled indicators orthonormal
for counting, so the standard dot product of coordinates
equals the counting product. The constant function is the sum
of the double-coset indicators. Its coordinate on a coset of
size #{s_j} is #{\sqrt{s_j}}. The function #{B} equals
#{\beta_j} on that coset, so its coordinate is
#{\beta_j\sqrt{s_j}}. \ref{fdip-012F} gives the value #{3}
exactly on the stabilizer. \ref{fdip-0114} identifies the
identity double coset with that stabilizer, and
\ref{fdip-0126} gives it order #{3}. The indicator of that
coset has coordinate #{\sqrt{3}} there and #{0} elsewhere.}

\p{Each size is positive. The real parts in \ref{fdip-012O} are
at least #{-1}, and \ref{fdip-012F} adds #{2}, so each value
of #{B} is at least #{1}. \ref{fdip-011X} makes every
coordinate of the eigenvector positive. Each of the three dot products is a sum of products
of positive terms, so each product is positive. A scalar
multiple of this eigenvector is either zero or nonzero in
every coordinate. The stabilizer indicator has a zero
coordinate, so it is not such a multiple. The constant
function and #{B} are nonzero in every coordinate, and the
products computed here do not decide whether either function
equals a scalar multiple of the eigenvector.}
}
